{
  "id": "Compound::DB00900 - Disease::DOID:635",
  "verbalization": "Didanosine, a nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor, is linked to Acquired Immunodeficiency Syndrome (AIDS) through a chain of biological relationships. The evidence suggests that Didanosine, as part of the broader class of nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors, is included in a category that includes compounds like Lamivudine and Abacavir, both of which are known to treat AIDS. This implies that Didanosine may exert its therapeutic effects through a mechanism that is similarly targeted at the virus responsible for AIDS, likely by inhibiting the enzyme reverse transcriptase, which is essential for HIV replication. The shared ancestry of Didanosine with these compounds, as indicated by their lowest common ancestors, suggests a common biological pathway or target, reinforcing the potential for Didanosine to be used in the treatment of AIDS. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors in the management of HIV, with Didanosine being a relevant member of this class.",
